A||||12||Cooter||Airbag danger? Anyone ever injured by one?||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||12-03-2000||06:04 PM||||My wife was in an accident yesterday. The car has a broked grill, headlights and bent hood. It did not even bend the fenders any. But, the airbags went off and the force caused her left hand to hit the door window and broke her arm! The impact from the actual wreck was so light that she is not even marked by the seat belt and the bag did not make contact anywhere on her except for her hands and tops of her legs. I am very thankful that she is not seriously injured, but the safety devices did more harm than good in this case. Has anyone else had an experience like this?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Hank is Here||12-03-2000||06:21 PM||||I drive tow truck and go to a lot of accidents, in general I see more good than bad with air bags. One thing in general when air bags deploy it seems most people get scratched in the face by the bag bucause the bag is not soft and it deploys at such a fast speed.  <P>One accident I remember well but not air bag related a fellow was killed.  It was an early 90's Pathfinder, fellow replaced the steering wheel with a Grant one. Well the grant wheel was a strong one, when he went into the wheel it did not give at all and basically mushed his internals, no blood all internal injuries.   I am rather sure if the stock wheel was in place it would have broken when he hit it and the fellow would still be here today.  I will never put an aftermarket wheel on a car, it is a part engineered to break!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||12-03-2000||06:33 PM||||I did a college research paper on air-bags not too long ago.  I found some some similar things, like drivers who had their arm crossed over the wheel (about to make a turn) resulted in broken arms when the air-bag went off.<P>Of the bad news, good to hear your wife will be ok.  It's unfortunate a device intended to save lives for those who don't wear seat belts caused yet another injury.||||reg||9
Z||000003||MrPBody||12-03-2000||06:51 PM||||You're sure to get lots of opinion on this one!<P>I have never owned a car with airbags, nor have I been in an airbag-deployment accident; however, I don't like them for many reasons:<P><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Airbags add weight, complexity, and failure potential.<BR><LI>Airbags are inflated by a small explosive charge; I'd rather not ride around with a bomb in my dashboard.<BR><LI>In a crash, airbags work <I>once</I>, giving no protection in repeated impacts or rollovers.<BR><LI>An airbag probably would smash my glasses.<BR><LI>You need to wear seatbelts with airbags; however, seatbelts work fine without airbags.  Why don't we just wear seatbelts?<BR></UL><P>OK, enough venting for one day.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-03-2000||07:15 PM||||There have been many many of these injured by air bad durring low speed crash incidents. Incidents where the car is hardly scratched and the driver or front seat passenger is injured or killed.<P>It a direct result of the air bag deploy sensor being set very low in american made cars. Be glad it wasn't certian modles of older Chryslers or she'd have been severly burned as well. There are allot of Chryslers running around where the airbag vents are on the sides. These bags have discharged hot inflator gasses directly onto drivers hands and arms in many accidents.<P><BR>The USDOT/NHTSA insurance industry and car makers are all aware of these problems. None of them really care. The car makers have done everything posible to delay or cancel alterations of the air bag system that would eliminate many of these injuries.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||12-03-2000||09:14 PM||||My grandmother drives a Buick (who woulda thought) Century.  She had to get her airbag turned off. She is short and fat so she has to sit with the seat all the way up.  And the airbag would kill her in an accident.  <P>The thing I found surprising was that its illegal in this state to get them turned off, she had to pay someone on the side to put in a switch that would turn it on and off.  <P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||hugh||12-03-2000||09:41 PM||||I do transport refrigeration and had one of my customers trucks in a while back to switch the body and refrigeration onto a newer chassis.The original was totaled.It was a Nisson Frontier,The air bag never deployed when it hit a pole with it's right fender.I was told the trigger assembly was on the left behind the bumper and was never tripped.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||12-03-2000||10:25 PM||||Black88- (Trying not to make my wife mad with this response  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ) but your grandmother shares my wife's shape and size. That is why I was surprised that the bag did not hit her chest because for her height, she has VERY short legs and a long "middle", so the seat was all the way forward. <BR>BTW- '95 Mercury Mystique||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black88GT||12-04-2000||12:22 AM||||Hmm Cooter thats weird.  I would have thought she would have been hurt more then that.  Now that I think about it they probably design airbags so no matter how short or fat you are you won't get hurt. <P>My grandmother is really short though, my uncle had to put wooden blocks on the pedals so she could reach them (not joking) LOL.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||frontal lobe||12-04-2000||01:28 AM||||Cooter,  I am glad your wife will be all right.  <P>I don't think it's fair to blame the car manufacturers on this one.  Reportedly, they BEGGED the NHTSA to not make them use such a rapid detonation in their airbags.  They were looking for a slower, safer rate of detonation such as used in Europe.  They KNEW arms would be broken.  They KNEW short people would be at risk, because it has to be set at some height and apparently it is set for the "average" 5'9" person.  But the NHTSA made them set it at the current settings, which is to protect an UNSEATBELTED occupant from a FRONTAL impact.  Those of us that wear our seatbelts and would be protected by that alone from all but the most horrendous accident are just out of luck if the bag deploys and injures us when it had no chance of providing us benefit.<P>I HATE airbags (as they are now).  I HATE motorized seatbelts.  There are some excellent safety mandates, though.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||12-04-2000||10:41 AM||||Cooter, <BR>I'm glad your wife was not injured more seriously. <BR>I remember hearing about a situation, a few months ago, regarding this sort of thing. Seems like there were numerous cases of Chevy Cavaliers (not sure about this, but it seems right) that had air bags deploy with only minor impacts, or <I>when they hit bumps in the road</I>.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  Seems like there were lawsuits involved. <BR>My wife has a 98 Jeep Wrangler. Supposedly, the airbags have been "tamed" a bit, from what they were in previous years. I hope we never have to find out. <BR>Best of luck to your wife.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cooter||12-05-2000||08:38 PM||||My wife said her ABS must have worked because she did not think the brakes locked up. The only problem is that the car does NOT have ABS! When I got the car today and tried to move it, I found that the brake pedal is BROKE! It just swings down to about an inch from the floor and then hits the pushrod. Anybody know a good lawyer? ||||reg||1
Z||000012||WampusCat||12-06-2000||09:39 AM||||I copied this from the cars.com website, very interesting, if long, article on their site under "advice"/safety.<P>The NHTSA reports that since 1990, airbag deployment has killed 169 people in low-severity crashes, including 63 drivers, six adult passengers, 82 children between the ages of 1 and 11, and 18 infants. Of the 63 adult drivers killed, 23 were women under 5 feet 2 inches tall, and three of the six adult passengers killed were females smaller than that height.  <p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 12-06-2000).]||||reg||1
